      {
        "id": "p34-challenge-q5-part-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Evaluate $2^8+2^{11}+2^n$ for $n$ between 1 and 8.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2306,\\ 2308,\\ 2312,\\ 2320,\\ 2336,\\ 2368,\\ 2432,\\ 2560$, respectively.",
          "worked": "$$2^8+2^{11}=256+2048=2304.$$ \nFor $n=1,2,\\ldots,8$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2304+2^1&=2306\\\\2304+2^2&=2308\\\\2304+2^3&=2312\\\\2304+2^4&=2320\\\\2304+2^5&=2336\\\\2304+2^6&=2368\\\\2304+2^7&=2432\\\\2304+2^8&=2560\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-challenge-q5-part-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the value of $n>8$ such that $2^8+2^{11}+2^n$ is a perfect square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=12$.\n\nThe sum is $6400=80^2$.",
          "worked": "Continue testing integer values of $n$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2304+2^9&=2816\\\\2304+2^{10}&=3328\\\\2304+2^{11}&=4352\\\\2304+2^{12}&=6400=80^2\\end{align*}$$\nThe first three values are not perfect squares, so $n=12$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
